  "excerpt_en": "Capturing website data precisely is essential for effective tracking. In e-commerce in particular, it is important to read information such as revenue from the data layer and define it as a variable in Google Tag Manager",
  "searchText_en": "Capturing website data precisely is essential for effective tracking. In e-commerce in particular, it is important to read information such as revenue from the data layer and define it as a variable in Google Tag Manager (GTM). This process can be made easier by using AI assistants such as ChatGPT, Copilot or Gemini. Keep in mind that the structure of the data layer varies depending on the content management system (CMS). The required variables therefore always have to be adapted to the specific system. If relevant information is not yet available in the data layer, it needs to be enabled in the CMS or added manually by the webmaster. The challenge is often understanding how the data layer is built and identifying and accessing specific values within its structure. AI assistants can make this step much easier. Step-by-step guide to defining variables with AI support: 1. Start preview mode in Google Tag Manager Open Google Tag Manager and turn on preview mode (top right, \"Preview\" button). Enter the URL of the website. 2. Trigger the conversion and analyze the data layer Navigate your website via the preview URL and trigger the desired conversion (e.g. an order). Then switch back to the GTM preview and look in the Data layer tab for the relevant information (e.g. the revenue value of a purchase event). 3. Extract the data layer for the AI Once you have found the information in the data layer, copy the entire content under Data layer values after this message . 4. Use an AI to define the variable Open an AI of your choice (e.g. ChatGPT, Copilot, Gemini) and paste the copied data layer into the chat. Use a precise prompt, for example: What should the data layer variable in Google Tag Manager be in order to get the revenue information? Replace revenue with the specific value you want to read (e.g. \"Transaction_ID\", \"PageType\" etc.). The AI will then give you the correct path to the variable in the data layer (e.g. eventModel.ecomm_totalvalue ). 5. Create the data layer variable in Google Tag Manager In Google Tag Manager, go to Variables (in the navigation on the left). In the User-defined variables section, click the \"New\" button. Give your variable a unique name (e.g. Revenue). Click the Variable configuration area. In the menu that appears, select the variable type Data layer variable under \"Page variables\". Copy the AI's output (e.g. eventModel.ecomm_totalvalue ) exactly into the Data layer variable name field. Save the variable. 6. Validate the variable in preview mode Start preview mode again and trigger the target event on your website once more. In the GTM preview, the newly defined variable should now appear under the name you gave it in the Variables section and show the correct value (e.g. the revenue for the purchase event). 7. Use the variable in tags If the variable shows the correct value, it can be used in the relevant tags (e.g. a Universal Analytics event tag, a Google Ads conversion tag or a Microsoft Advertising tag). In GTM, go to Tags and find the tag you want to edit. Find the corresponding field for the newly defined variable (e.g. \"revenue value\" for revenue). Click the building-block icon next to the field and select the name of the newly defined variable. The variable is then stored in the field as: {{variable name}} . 8. Publish the changes Finally, publish the changes in Google Tag Manager to activate the new variables for your tags and pass the additional information (such as revenue) to the respective trackers."
